-- map_areas_data.lua contains bundled reference data. See its schema below and CODE_GUIDE.md.
-- Used by: map_areas.lua.
--[=[
BEGINNER WALKTHROUGH: map_areas_data.lua

The outer dictionary is keyed by map AREA ID; numeric key 0 is valid.
An area has id/name, displayed bounds x/z/w/h in world tiles, segments (coordinate/floor
transforms), points (map pin records) and labels ({text,x,z} tuples).
Each segment uses sx/sz for source origin, dx/dz for display origin, w/h for world size,
level for first source floor and levels for how many floors it accepts.

Example reuse through the checked public helpers:
  local areas = require('map_areas')
  local cavern = areas.Get(753)
  local x, z = areas.Project(753, 3296, 7610, 0)
  if x then print(cavern.name, x, z) end
map_areas.lua allows only native_map_list IDs; area_tiles_data uses these SAME IDs for images.
A usable new area needs its definition, allow-list entry, tile metadata AND image resources.

Cross-file connections:
  No require dependencies inside this file.
  Required directly by: map_areas.lua.

require normally caches a module after the first load. A data module returns values;
a helper module returns functions/state constructors. See CODE_GUIDE.md for the full flow.
]=]

--[[
Bundled non-surface area definitions and cached marker/label records.
Schema: [native area ID] = {id, name, x, z, w, h, segments, points, labels}.
Segments contain source sx/sz, destination dx/dz, size w/h and level/levels; all are world
tile/floor units. points use projected map coordinates; labels are {text, x, z} tuples.
map_areas limits selectable entries via native_map_list. Scabarite area 753 is restored
explicitly from its existing cached base image. Cached geography is not live terrain.
]]

